# Documentation Standards (Quick Reference)

Documentation enables auditors to understand **intent**, not just function. Full templates and examples: see `documentation-standards-full.mdc`.

## JSDoc (all exported functions)

- **Required:** `@description`, `@param` (each param), `@returns` (if not void), `@throws` (error conditions)
- **When relevant:** `@security` (auth/security code), `@audit` (audit-logged functions)

## Security comment prefixes (grep-able)

- `// SECURITY:` — security-sensitive logic
- `// AUTH:` — authentication/authorization
- `// AUDIT:` — audit logging
- `// RLS:` — row-level security notes
- `// VALIDATION:` — input validation
- `// SENSITIVE:` — handling sensitive data (e.g. PII)

## Business logic

- Comment the **WHY**, not the WHAT. Reference tickets (PRODUCT-xxx, INCIDENT-date) when relevant.
- No commented-out code; no TODO/FIXME without ticket reference.

## Components

- JSDoc with `@description` and `@example`. Document each prop in the props interface.

## File headers

Required for: API routes (auth/payments), files > 200 lines, security-critical logic, DB migrations. Include @file, @description, @security/@audit as applicable.

## Decision tree

Security/auth? → AUTH/SECURITY. Audit logging? → AUDIT. Non-obvious why? → Explain. Business rule? → Context/ticket. Else → usually no comment.
